# Paths here are relative to the project root, and the package lives under
# src/. These lines used to say `intellipandora/conf` -- the *distribution*
# name, not the package name (`ipandora`) nor its real path -- so they matched
# nothing at all, and two of the four lines named directories
# (`intellipandora/statistics`, `conf/pytest_template`) that do not exist.
#
# On its own that was not what broke the published wheels. Current setuptools
# (tested at 79.0.1) honours `include_package_data=True` by taking every file
# inside a package directory, so the data files ship here even with MANIFEST.in
# deleted outright -- which is why the 1.1.0 wheel on PyPI carries
# conf/__init__.py and nothing beside it, while a build from this same source
# today carries all of them. The artifact depended on the setuptools version of
# whatever machine ran the release.
#
# So this file is now correct, but correctness here is not the guarantee.
# `scripts/check_packaging.py` is: it looks inside the built wheel. It fails
# the real 1.1.0 wheel and passes a build from this tree.
recursive-include src/ipandora/conf *
global-include CHANGELOG
global-include AUTHORS.rst
global-exclude __pycache__
global-exclude *.py[co]
global-exclude .gitignore

# Key material. The framework writes its own AES/Fernet key on first use; it
# now lands in ~/.ipandora/crypto, but a stale copy under conf/crypto from an
# older checkout must never be packaged. Given the behaviour above -- data
# files inside a package get picked up whatever this file says -- these
# excludes matter more than the include does.
global-exclude *.pem
global-exclude *.key
prune src/ipandora/conf/crypto
